WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:03.360
- You mentioned, in an interview,

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.470
that science is not a collection of facts

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:07.610
but a constant human struggle

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:09.203
with the mysteries of nature.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:12.880
What was the first mystery
that you can recall

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:13.810
that hooked you?

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:14.906
That captivated-

00:00:14.906 --> 00:00:17.133
- Oh the first mystery, that's a good one.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.703
Yeah, I remember that.

00:00:21.503 --> 00:00:22.336
- What was it?

00:00:22.336 --> 00:00:23.886
- I had a fever for three days.

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:29.283
When I learned about Descartes
and a little geometry.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:33.540
And I found out that you
can do all the construction

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.523
in geometry using algebra.

00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:38.160
And I couldn't get over it.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.550
I simply couldn't get out of bed.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:42.440
I- (laughs)

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:45.603
- What kind of world does
analytical geometry unlock?

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:49.293
- Well, it connects algebra with geometry.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:53.480
Okay, so Descartes had the idea that

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:58.050
geometrical construction
and geometrical theorems

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:01.190
and their assumptions can be articulated

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:03.210
in the language of algebra.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:07.000
Which means that all
the proofs that we did

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.660
in high school...

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:13.660
Trying to prove that the three
bisectors meet at one point

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:16.013
and that (laughs) okay.

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:19.710
All this can be proven by-

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:20.543
- Pure algebra.

00:01:20.543 --> 00:01:23.140
- Shuffling around notation.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:25.100
Yeah that was a traumatic experience.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:25.950
- The connection-

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:28.140
(laughs) Traumatic experience.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:28.973
- For me, it was.

00:01:28.973 --> 00:01:29.941
I'm telling you.

00:01:29.941 --> 00:01:31.560
- So it's the connection
between the different

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:32.800
mathematical disciplines?

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:34.113
That they all-

00:01:34.113 --> 00:01:35.593
- (mumbles) to different languages.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:37.480
- Languages.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:38.313
- Yeah.

00:01:38.313 --> 00:01:40.850
- So which mathematic
discipline is most beautiful?

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:42.250
Is geometry it for you?

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:44.360
- Both are beautiful, they have

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:46.090
almost the same power.

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:48.620
- But there's a visual
element to geometry, being-

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:49.453
- Visual...

00:01:49.453 --> 00:01:51.760
It's more transparent.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.890
But once you get over to algebra then

00:01:56.230 --> 00:01:58.080
linear equations, straight line,

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.653
this translation is easily absorbed.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:06.121
And to pass a tangent to a circle,

00:02:06.121 --> 00:02:08.610
you know, you have the basic theorems

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:11.410
and you can do it with algebra.

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:15.230
But the transition from
one to another was really-

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:17.820
I thought that Descartes was
the greatest mathematician

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:18.883
of all times.

